        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br> 
The Artificial Neural Networks and Cardiovascular Regulation experiment was one of four autonomic nervous system experiments performed during the 1998 Neurolab mission.  This experiment, in conjunction with the other three ANS experiments, focused on how the cardiovascular system adapts to microgravity and neural connection changes (plasticity) to cope with orthostatic stress. 
<P>
By studying dynamic response of the cardiovascular system to both microgravity and readaptation to 1g, the investigator hoped to fulfill one of his main objectives, which was to construct a model of cardiovascular regulation.  This model of artificial neural networks has many benefits once constructed, for better understanding of human physiology in space and on Earth.  One-half million Americans suffer from disorders of autonomic blood pressure control that often result in orthostatic intolerance. Orthostatic intolerance is a condition of lightheadedness, possibly leading to fainting, that occurs when an upright position is assumed. It is caused by low blood pressure and an inadequate supply of blood reaching the brain. Astronauts often experience temporary orthostatic intolerance when remaining upright after returning from stays in space since their blood volume is reduced in weightlessness and the pull of gravity does not allow blood to reach their heads. 
<P> 
Through characterization of this phenomenon, investigators aimed to identify the mechanisms underlying orthostatic intolerance and cardiovascular disregulation in general. Information from this experiment can support non-invasive assessment of neurohumoral cardiovascular regulation at a pre-clinical level and may contribute to disease prevention in patients. 
<P>   
APPROACH: <br> 
As part of the Autonomic team's experiments, a Lower Body Negative Pressure (LBNP) device was used to challenge the autonomic nervous system.  LBNP places stress on an astronaut's cardiovascular system, very similar to standing in Earth's gravity.  LBNP creates orthostatic stress, in that the term "orthostatic" relates to the upright position of the body.  In association with orthostatic intolerence, orthostatic hypotension is a temporary condition of low blood pressure found in some people when they stand upright.  In this particular device, air is sucked out a mylar bag to a level of -15 mmHg and -30 mmHg, causing blood to pool in the astronaut's lower body region.   
<p>
Prior to LBNP suction, however, a cuff is used on the astronaut's thigh to stress that portion of the subject's body, while measurements are made to determine fluid distribution.  Using Body Impedance Measurement (BIM) electrodes, the electrical impedance of the body is measured, providing body fluid distribution information.  Similar results are determined via Applied Potential Tomography (APT), which uses electrical resistivity.  The APT belt is positioned under the thigh cuff, which was inflated to 180 mmHg for two minutes, followed by a five-minute deflation period and a two-minute recovery period.  So in conjunction with LBNP, APT measures volume changes in the leg while the leg is subjected to negative pressure.  These measurements, together with cuff occlusion and BIM, show fluid distribution in the body and how this may contribute to blood pressure control problems. 
<P> 
While these methods are used inflight, a similar protocol is performed pre- and post- flight.  In addition tilt tests are done before and after flight, in which a table is tilted to 60 degrees for 10 minutes, then the subject is returned to the supine position for recovery.  During this time, cardiac output and cuff blood pressure measurements are made to obtain a baseline for each subject.   
<P> 
RESULTS: <br> 
All three scheduled sessions were performed by the four payload crewmembers, one session early (Flight Day 2), the second session mid (Flight Days 7 and 8), and the third session late (Flight Days 12 and 13) in flight.  However, the APT signal in the mid-mission session was lost, so the protocol was repeated for two subjects on Flight Day 10.  Additional sessions were performed on Flight Days 2 and 8 by one subject on the early mission session.  Data appear to be of good quality based on the initial evaluation. <P> 
Reductions of cerebral blood flow velocity during lower body suction were significant before space flight (P < 0.05, repeated measures ANOVA), but not during or after space flight. Absolute and percentage reductions of mean (+/- s.e.m.) cerebral blood flow velocity after 10 minutes upright tilt were smaller after than before space flight (absolute, -4 +/- 3 cm per sec after versus -14 +/- 3 cm per sec before, P = 0.001; and percentage, -8.0 +/- 4.8% after versus -24.8 +/- 4.4% before, P < 0.05), consistent with improved rather than impaired cerebral blood flow regulation. Low-frequency gain decreased significantly (P < 0.05) by 26, 23 and 27% after 1 and 2 weeks in space and on landing day, respectively, compared with preflight values, which is also consistent with improved autoregulation. It can be concluded that human cerebral autoregulation is preserved, and possibly even improved, by short-duration space flight.
<p>
